data.content:
`SafeAreaEdges="None"` only tells MAUI not to add safe-area padding to the page layout. You likely still need to:

1. **Hide Shell/Navigation chrome** (if using Shell):
   ```xml
   <ContentPage ...
       Shell.NavBarIsVisible="False"
       Shell.TabBarIsVisible="False"
       SafeAreaEdges="None">
   ```

2. **On Android — enable edge-to-edge mode** so content draws behind system bars. In your `MainActivity.cs`:
   ```csharp
   protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
   {
       base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

       if (Window != null)
       {
           Window.SetStatusBarColor(Android.Graphics.Color.Transparent);
           Window.SetNavigationBarColor(Android.Graphics.Color.Transparent);
           WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window, false);
       }
   }
   ```
   You'll need `using AndroidX.Core.View;`.

3. **On iOS** — `SafeAreaEdges="None"` should be sufficient, but ensure the page's `Padding` isn't set and the `Image` uses:
   ```xml
   <Image Source="photo.jpg" Aspect="AspectFill"
          HorizontalOptions="Fill" VerticalOptions="Fill" />
   ```

4. **Ensure no inherited Padding/Margin** — check that neither the page nor any parent layout has explicit `Padding` or `Margin`.

The most commonly missed piece is step 2 — without `SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window, false)`, Android won't let content render behind the system bars regardless of MAUI layout settings.
